course: dictionary
lesson: 12452
title: A word that can be undone
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words are big and rare, but you can work them out by reading the words around them. Defeasible sounds a bit like defeat, and that is a useful clue: it means able to be cancelled or overturned by something new. «The library card was defeasible: it could be cancelled if the book was lost.» The card works, but only until a new fact undoes it.
explain: Lawyers use this word for rights and promises that can be taken away if something happens. «My right to play in the park is defeasible: it ends if I break the rules.» Thinkers who study logic use it for good guesses. «Birds fly, so Tweety flies» is a good guess, but it is defeasible: learning that Tweety is a penguin overturns it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "able to be cancelled" in «The club's membership was able to be cancelled by a new vote.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Defeasible means able to be cancelled or overturned.
fact: Defeasible is an adjective, and it is not compared, so we do not say "more defeasible".
fact: In law, a defeasible right can be taken away if a condition happens.
fact: In logic, a defeasible conclusion is a good guess that new evidence could overturn.
fact: A defeasible conclusion is true unless shown otherwise.
exercise: «The club's membership was defeasible: a new vote could cancel it.» What does defeasible mean? | *Able to be cancelled or overturned | Certain to last forever | Very big and strong | Written down in gold
exercise: «Birds fly, so Tweety flies» is a defeasible conclusion. Why? | It is a word for a loud bird | It is a fact about all animals | *New facts, such as Tweety being a penguin, could overturn it | It can never be changed by anything
exercise: «The right was defeasible, so it could be taken away.» What does the second half of the sentence tell you about defeasible? | *It can be cancelled | It is very loud | It is a type of food | It is always permanent
test: «A defeasible contract can end if one side breaks a promise.» What does defeasible suggest here? | The contract is written in a language nobody knows | *The contract can be cancelled | The contract is very long | The contract lasts forever
test: Which of these is a defeasible conclusion? | Fish is a word with four letters | Mimi is a cat, so Mimi is a cat | *Most cats like fish, so Mimi probably likes fish, until we learn otherwise | Cats are sleepy
exam: «Our plan was defeasible: a single new fact could undo it.» What does defeasible mean? | Very heavy | *Able to be undone | Certain and fixed | Written in capital letters
